    "remaining_conditions": [
      "The ordinary later-main rows compare the direct pinned-C `_mi_thread_init_with_heap(mi_heap_main())` caller's Malloc TLD/Theap, dual list, default/fixed-TLS root, counter and explicit-finish post-state with Rust's `MainHeapThreadAttachment` natively on AArch64. The C fixtures separately verify source order, which Rust does not alias, and the paired Theap-failure row compares only retained failure post-state under source-specific C and typed Rust faults. Every other pinned later-main caller enters the same body through `_mi_thread_init()` and `_mi_thread_init_with_heap(NULL)`, which selects `mi_heap_main()` (src/init.c:305-369): an uninitialized thread's first allocation (src/page.c:1011-1020), `mi_heap_init_theap` (src/heap.c:67-69), `mi_heap_new_in_arena` (src/heap.c:150-155), `mi_theap_get_default` (src/theap.c:172-180), `mi_subproc_new` (src/subproc.c:158-159) and public `mi_thread_init`. Rust has no lazy later-thread entry: crabc attaches each worker before user code, and a native allocation from an unregistered thread after startup is refused (crabc-mimalloc/src/runtime_lifecycle.rs `enter_native_first_allocation_operation`), so those callers have no Rust receiver. `mi_subproc_add_current_thread`'s child-Heap caller (src/subproc.c:284-300) is outside these rows.",
      "Automatic thread and process teardown is qualified on AArch64 by the pinned-C automatic, cancellation and process-done pthread-key receipts and by the Rust owner-exit, physical process-destroy and fork-repair owners, all executed natively here. Their installed-product composition through crabc libc (natural return, pthread_exit, cancellation, fork and the .fini_array process-done) is runtime-lane evidence, not this allocator gate's.",
      "Repeated explicit process initialization matches pinned C natively on AArch64 both on the component storage (the explicit-worker-recovery receipt) and through the native runtime entry `initialize_process` with a distinct caller (src/init.c:537-592, src/libc.c:115-140). The loader-tail release boundary and the first-allocation entropy fault also match. In pinned C a callback runs inside the process body only when it was registered before `mi_process_init` (src/options.c:424-428); otherwise body messages enter the delayed buffer (src/options.c:365-382) that the loader tail flushes. Rust admits no output registration before startup (crabc-mimalloc/src/source_options_api.rs `register_output`) and routes startup reservation warnings through the published process output (crabc-mimalloc/src/process_init.rs `process_warning_message`), so its process-body interval with the blocked distinct caller stays the test-component startup's controlled post-attachment observer, and the delivery reachable in the product is the loader-tail flush. The Rust-only startup rows remain ownership evidence; installed-product startup is runtime-lane evidence.",
      "Repeat this component's native evidence on 16 KiB and 64 KiB base-page AArch64 hosts; the executed receivers ran on the qualifying arm64 VM's 4 KiB base pages."
